{0 Owebview}

A thin OCaml binding to {{:https://github.com/webview/webview}webview}: open a
native window backed by the operating system's web engine, load HTML/CSS/JS,
and bridge JavaScript with OCaml — no Electron, no bundler.

{1 Two libraries}

The [owebview] package installs two libraries, and the split is deliberate.

- {b [owebview]} — module {!module:Webview}. The binding proper: the complete
  webview 0.12 C API and nothing else. It stays thin on purpose, and leaves
  higher-level conveniences (JSON for the bridge, for instance) to the caller.

- {b [owebview.desktop]} — module {!module:Webview_desktop}. Everything a
  native application needs {e around} the web view: finding its assets on
  disk, its icon in the Dock, the system's modal dialogs, the menu bar. None
  of it is part of the webview API — it calls Cocoa, GTK and Win32 directly,
  and its C stubs include no part of [webview.h]. It reaches the window
  through {!Webview.get_window}, like any other user of the binding could.

Depend on the first alone for a window whose content is an inline HTML string;
add the second as soon as the page comes from files on disk:

{[
  (executable
   (name main)
   (libraries owebview owebview.desktop))
]}

{1 Quickstart}

A whole application is about ten lines:

{[
  let () =
    let w = Webview.create () in
    Webview.set_title w "My first owebview app";
    Webview.set_size w ~width:480 ~height:320 Webview.Hint_none;
    Webview.set_html w "<h1>Hello from OCaml</h1>";
    Webview.run w;
    Webview.destroy w
]}

Call OCaml from the page and answer the JavaScript promise:

{[
  Webview.bind w "add" (fun id req ->
      let result =
        match Scanf.sscanf_opt req "[%d,%d]" (fun a b -> a + b) with
        | Some n -> string_of_int n
        | None -> "null"
      in
      Webview.return w id ~error:false ~result)
]}

Load a real page, with its CSS and images, from a directory next to the
executable:

{[
  let index =
    Filename.concat (Webview_desktop.Locate_assets.web_dir ()) "index.html"
  in
  Webview.navigate w ("file://" ^ index)
]}

A step-by-step
{{:https://github.com/korkorran/Owebview/tree/main/tutorial}tutorial} builds
this up from a first window to a complete application.

{1 [owebview]: the webview binding}

The whole binding is the {!module:Webview} module. A tour of it:

- {b Lifecycle}: {!Webview.create}, {!Webview.run}, {!Webview.terminate},
  {!Webview.destroy}
- {b Window}: {!Webview.set_title}, {!Webview.set_size}
- {b Content}: {!Webview.navigate}, {!Webview.set_html}, {!Webview.init},
  {!Webview.eval}
- {b JavaScript bridge}: {!Webview.bind}, {!Webview.return},
  {!Webview.unbind}
- {b Threading}: {!Webview.dispatch}, the one safe way to drive the window
  from another thread
- {b Native handles}: {!Webview.get_window}, {!Webview.get_native_handle}

{1 [owebview.desktop]: native desktop integration}

Four independent modules under {!module:Webview_desktop}:

- {!module:Webview_desktop.Locate_assets} — find the application's [web/]
  directory relative to the {e executable} rather than the current working
  directory, so a page loads the same under [dune exec], from an installed
  binary, or from a Dock icon. {!Webview_desktop.Locate_assets.web_dir} is the
  one to call.

- {!module:Webview_desktop.Icon} — give a plain executable a real icon
  instead of the generic one: {!Webview_desktop.Icon.set_app_icon} for the
  Dock, taskbar or switcher, and {!Webview_desktop.Icon.set_app_id} for the
  application id a Wayland desktop matches against an installed [.desktop]
  file.

- {!module:Webview_desktop.Dialog} — the system's own modal dialogs:
  {!Webview_desktop.Dialog.confirm}, {!Webview_desktop.Dialog.open_file} and
  {!Webview_desktop.Dialog.open_directory}. They block until the user answers
  and must run on the UI thread, which makes a binding callback their natural
  home.

- {!module:Webview_desktop.Menu} — the native menu bar, described as an
  ordinary OCaml value and installed with {!Webview_desktop.Menu.set}, with
  keyboard accelerators and per-item callbacks.

Each module's own page documents what differs between platforms, which is
where the real detail lives: macOS runs the menu bar and the Dock icon
application-globally, Linux needs a [.desktop] file under Wayland, and GTK 4
supports neither the dialogs nor the menu bar.

{1 Native dev dependencies}

webview uses the system web engine, so building against it needs that engine's
development files. Only Windows asks anything of you:

- {b macOS}: WebKit / Cocoa, already provided by the system. Nothing to
  install.
- {b Linux}: [gtk+-3.0] and [webkit2gtk-4.1] (the [-dev] packages). They come
  from the [conf-gtk3-webkit] opam package, so [opam install] offers to
  install them for your distribution.
- {b Windows}: the WebView2 SDK headers, which are not redistributable and so
  are not vendored. Fetch them once with
  [nuget install Microsoft.Web.WebView2]; the build finds [WebView2.h] in the
  NuGet cache on its own. The WebView2 runtime itself needs nothing — it ships
  with Windows 10 and 11.

[owebview.desktop] needs strictly less — the platform toolkit without the web
engine — so it adds nothing to install. The platform-specific compile and link
flags of both libraries are detected automatically at build time, and there is
nothing to tweak by hand.
